{"qid":"Q208404","names":["Kingdom of Armenia"],"title":"Kingdom of Armenia","endonym":{"native":"Մեծ Հայք","lang":"hy"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of Armenia was established around 331 BC when Alexander the Great's victory over Darius III broke Achaemenid power in the region, though a distinct Armenian kingdom solidified under the Artaxiad (Orontid successor) dynasty from 189 BC. It reached its greatest extent under Tigranes II the Great (r. 95–55 BC), who conquered Mesopotamia, Syria, and Cilicia to create an empire stretching from the Caspian to the Mediterranean, until Pompey's campaigns reduced him to a client king of Rome in 66 BC. Subsequent Armenian kings were caught between Roman and Parthian/Sasanian power as a buffer state; the Arsacid line converted to Christianity in 301 AD under Tiridates III (making Armenia the first state to adopt Christianity as its official religion), before the kingdom was partitioned between Byzantium and Sasanian Persia in 387 AD and the remaining eastern portion finally abolished in 428 AD.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial extent of Artaxiad-era Armenia is attested by Strabo, Plutarch, and Armenian chronicles, but precise boundaries, especially during Tigranes II's conquests, are reconstructed from narrative sources rather than survey documents; confidence is moderate for core territories, low for the maximum extent.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Armenia (antiquity)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Armenia_(antiquity)"},{"label":"Britannica: Armenia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Armenia"}],"britann